WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:05.100
You don't need insidious spirits, axe-wielding
psychos, or even vampires to make a movie

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.100
scary.

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.190
For some films, it's the wild imagery and
emotional and psychological impacts of the

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:13.430
pics that have made audiences shudder for
generations, despite never being labeled as

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:14.430
horror flicks.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:18.120
Here are some movies that have managed to
strike fear into viewers, no matter the genre

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.510
label.

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:21.110
Return to Oz

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:24.869
While The Wizard of Oz boasted some slightly
creepy moments, it was nothing compared to

00:00:24.869 --> 00:00:28.310
what craziness Return to Oz had to offer half
a century later.

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:33.230
Whoever decided a horrific depiction of electroshock
therapy, a talking disembodied moose head,

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:37.730
and sending a young Dorothy to an actual asylum
was a totally solid idea must've been friends

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:41.020
with the Scarecrow, because on paper it sounds
pretty brainless.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.660
"What do you think?"

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:48.760
What the film tries to pass off as whimsy
is utterly upsetting, with the creatures of

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:52.300
Oz 2.0 arriving as much more horrifying than
cute.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:56.610
The creatures of Oz in this pic range from
an unintentionally scary pumpkin-headed sidekick

00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:00.170
to the very intentionally scary villain, the
Nome King.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:03.260
And that's not even getting into the segment
in which we discover that the fun, light-hearted

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:06.330
sidekicks from the original film have been
turned to stone.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:08.270
Return to Oz isn't a bad movie, though.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:12.510
The creatures are still excellently designed
and puppeteered, and while the story is twisted,

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:14.000
it does have a lot of heart.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.170
And… a few dozen decapitated heads.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:21.860
"I believe I'll lock you in the tower for
a few years til your head is ready, and then

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:22.860
I'll take it."

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.260
Not terrifying at all.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:27.479
The Brave Little Toaster

00:01:27.479 --> 00:01:31.630
Before Toy Story launched a multimillion dollar
franchise on the concept of inanimate objects

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:35.060
coming to life when humans weren't looking,
there was The Brave Little Toaster.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:38.490
And if you find yourself wondering why the
former took off while the latter has drifted

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:42.550
into the distant plains of nostalgia, you
should rewatch the movie to remind yourself

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:47.090
that it's a technicolor nightmare of a film
that no child has, nor ever had, any business

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:48.090
watching.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:52.650
The themes ultimately boil down to a generational
shift in household appliances—one that is,

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:54.799
in retrospect, totally valid.

00:01:54.799 --> 00:01:58.770
Technology grows and gets updated, and it's
strange and unsettling that the filmmakers

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:03.509
insisted that a kid going off to college should
feel a sentimental attachment to his toaster.

00:02:03.509 --> 00:02:05.170
We all know where that leads, right?

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:06.590
"I love you."

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:11.079
And then when you do get into the nightmarish
imagery, it's a whole new level of inappropriately

00:02:11.079 --> 00:02:12.079
scary.

00:02:12.079 --> 00:02:16.469
Evil firefighter clowns, panic-induced hallucinations,
anatomically correct computer towers, and

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:20.689
a crushing sequence in which the appliance
gang finds themselves trapped in a junkyard

00:02:20.689 --> 00:02:24.109
make for an experience that even adults may
not escape mentally unscathed.

00:02:24.109 --> 00:02:26.320
"Did you hear that, boys?

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.600
They want to know how to escape!"

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.540
Requiem for a Dream

00:02:33.549 --> 00:02:37.049
Addiction is a very real horror millions of
people grapple with daily, and perhaps no

00:02:37.049 --> 00:02:41.419
film has captured it as poignantly as Darren
Aronofsky's bone-chilling Requiem for a Dream.

00:02:41.419 --> 00:02:45.659
Centering on a group of four people, it depicts
the various stages of addiction to drugs,

00:02:45.659 --> 00:02:47.519
including amphetamines and heroin.

00:02:47.519 --> 00:02:50.969
The film is unflinching in its portrayal of
the beginning stages of addiction, the side

00:02:50.969 --> 00:02:54.769
effects that come with it, and the withdrawal
symptoms and fallout from trying and failing

00:02:54.769 --> 00:02:55.769
to get clean.

00:02:55.769 --> 00:02:59.999
From self-debasement in search of one more
hit, to a full-on arm amputation, there's

00:02:59.999 --> 00:03:03.659
a lot to unpack from the results of this disease
and all its iterations.

00:03:03.659 --> 00:03:06.090
Aronofsky doesn't sugarcoat a single frame.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:08.359
Everything feels real; everything hurts.

00:03:08.359 --> 00:03:11.430
Every moment of the film is an agony-ridden
reminder of the anguish of addiction.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:15.339
It might not be a conventional horror film,
but it's scarier than almost any movie about

00:03:15.339 --> 00:03:17.639
ghosts or werewolves.

00:03:17.639 --> 00:03:19.629
The NeverEnding Story

00:03:19.629 --> 00:03:20.909
Fear the Nothing, kids.

00:03:20.909 --> 00:03:24.790
The NeverEnding Story has some frightening
imagery—but it stands out as particularly

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:29.280
eerie thanks to the fact that its villain
is a bone-chilling avatar of nihilism.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.670
Young heroes Bastian and Atreyu are trying
to save the land of Fantasia from the Nothing,

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:35.980
a spreading of literal nonexistence.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:36.980
It's not a monster.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:39.510
It doesn't have fangs or claws or horrible
powers.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:42.140
It's just a void spreading across the land.

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:44.610
Everything it touches exists until it suddenly
doesn't.

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:48.339
Then, there's the horrors of depression and
grief, which is rammed into our brains with

00:03:48.339 --> 00:03:50.879
one devastating scene involving a horse.

00:03:50.879 --> 00:03:57.369
"We don't even care whether or not we care."

00:03:57.369 --> 00:04:01.619
These are big concepts for kids to comprehend,
but by sweeping away all of our new fantasy

00:04:01.619 --> 00:04:06.870
pals and threatening to destroy the childlike
empress, it's shockingly scary all the same.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:08.139
Cropsey

00:04:08.139 --> 00:04:09.619
Where do urban legends come from?

00:04:09.619 --> 00:04:13.540
To find answers, the filmmakers behind the
documentary Cropsey look into the origins

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:17.789
of the titular New York City boogeyman, a
sort of amalgam of archetypes like the escaped

00:04:17.789 --> 00:04:20.209
inmate and the scary man living in the woods.

00:04:20.209 --> 00:04:24.270
What they find is not a concrete answer as
to whether Cropsey was ever real, but rather

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:28.930
a sprawling investigation into a series of
child kidnappings in the '70s and '80s, with

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:31.610
more questions springing up every time they
find an answer.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:35.771
The truth, as they find, is not only stranger
than fiction but infinitely more gut-wrenching.

00:04:35.771 --> 00:04:40.020
"You've got this guy, going around, picking
off all these kids."

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:45.070
The kidnapper, Andre Rand, was found guilty
of kidnapping two of five missing people attributed

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:46.389
to him, mostly children.

00:04:46.389 --> 00:04:50.490
He's currently in prison but was never convicted
of anything more serious, as four of the bodies

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:51.580
were never found.

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:55.580
Cropsey is a tragically poignant look at the
relationship between truth and fiction, and

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:59.449
while he may still be locked up, the phantom
of these horrific crimes still lives on to

00:04:59.449 --> 00:05:00.919
this day.

00:05:00.919 --> 00:05:02.150
Akira

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:07.430
Katsuhiro Otomo's legendary magnum opus is
lauded for its groundbreaking animation, design,

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:08.430
and depth.

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:11.830
What occasionally gets overlooked is the fact
that it's a very frightening film.

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:15.900
Exploring the fallout of nuclear war and the
aftermath of Tokyo being razed in an atomic

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:20.819
blast, Akira tells the story of bike gangs,
psychic children, and the evils of the military

00:05:20.819 --> 00:05:22.150
industrial complex.

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:25.560
The subject matter is scary enough, but what
really comes down to making it grotesquely

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.180
terrifying is the visuals.

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:31.889
From a giant oozing teddy bear to a psychic
prodigy's body growing and mutating like microwaved

00:05:31.889 --> 00:05:34.720
Play-Doh, Akira is riddled with visual horrors.

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:38.750
The film is a spectacular achievement in animation
and science fiction, but don't sleep on how

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:39.800
scary it is.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:43.819
Pairing a grim look at humanity's future with
graphic imagery, this movie will shake you

00:05:43.819 --> 00:05:45.960
to your core.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:46.960
Matilda

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:49.289
Why is Matilda a low-key terrifying movie?

00:05:49.289 --> 00:05:52.009
Three words: Principal Agatha Trunchbull.

00:05:52.009 --> 00:05:55.379
"Your mommy is a TWIT!"

00:05:55.379 --> 00:05:58.670
When removed from its villain, it's a fun
little film about a brilliant young girl with

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:01.689
psychic powers navigating a world that doesn't
understand her.

00:06:01.689 --> 00:06:04.840
But throw in Principal Trunchbull and you've
got something much darker.

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:13.960
Trunchbull is sadistic, clever, and almost
animalistic in her aggression towards her

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:14.960
students.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:18.220
She also implicitly murdered the father of
Ms. Honey, Matilda's teacher and the one person

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:19.620
who seems to understand her.

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:23.310
Any time a child protagonist is put up against
an adult villain, there's a certain power

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:25.849
dynamic that makes the relationship inherently
disturbing.

00:06:25.849 --> 00:06:29.810
But when viewers are presented with a character
as fundamentally good and pure as Matilda

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:34.100
and a villain as monstrous and borderline
inhuman as Trunchbull, it becomes genuinely

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:35.100
ghastly.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:38.370
For evidence, look no further than the scene
in which Matilda and Ms. Honey sneak into

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:40.310
Trunchbull's house and almost get caught.

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:44.449
Matilda is a great movie as it is, but the
addition of Agatha Trunchbull not only makes

00:06:44.449 --> 00:06:47.120
it better, it makes it scary.

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:48.599
No Country for Old Men

00:06:48.599 --> 00:06:52.370
At the pitch-black heart of the Coen brothers'
adaptation of Cormac McCarthy's No Country

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:57.979
for Old Men is Javier Bardem as Anton Chigurh,
a restrained, lurking hulk of a man on a mission

00:06:57.979 --> 00:06:59.229
of anarchy and evil.

00:06:59.229 --> 00:07:02.690
His performance earned him an Oscar and gave
life to one of the definitive villains of

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:03.740
a generation.

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:08.560
Physically unremarkable save for a truly horrible
haircut, Chigurh trudges unrelentingly through

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:13.080
the streets, a hit man assigned to take the
life of Josh Brolin's Llewelyn Moss.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:17.139
Despite his uncannily quiet demeanor, Chigurh
is a human wrecking ball, slaughtering and

00:07:17.139 --> 00:07:20.009
maiming indiscriminately as he makes his way
to Moss.

00:07:20.009 --> 00:07:23.029
He resembles less a human and more a force
of nature.

00:07:23.029 --> 00:07:27.150
Bardem's performance is restrained when necessary
but brutal and thunderous when the right moment

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:28.150
comes.

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:32.080
He's the lynchpin of an already stellar film,
a masterpiece of tension and inevitability.

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:35.939
Moss is doomed as soon as Chigurh has his
name—the horror comes in watching him try

00:07:35.939 --> 00:07:40.419
to avoid a fate the audience immediately recognizes
as inevitable.

00:07:40.419 --> 00:07:42.590
Willy Wonka and the Chocolate Factory

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:46.150
Although it's widely regarded as a children's
film, Willy Wonka and the Chocolate Factory

00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:49.910
is a movie that cranks the scare factor up
to ten with astoundingly little warning.

00:07:49.910 --> 00:07:54.449
At first, it's a fun, inspirational movie
about a boy named Charlie getting the opportunity

00:07:54.449 --> 00:07:57.289
to tour a magnificent, magical candy factory.

00:07:57.289 --> 00:07:58.289
Sounds fun, right?

00:07:58.289 --> 00:08:00.949
Then Charlie goes to the actual factory and
things get weird.

00:08:00.949 --> 00:08:03.289
"This is going to be such an exciting day.

00:08:03.289 --> 00:08:05.130
I hope you enjoy it.

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:06.389
I think you will."

00:08:06.389 --> 00:08:10.309
The next thing you know, candymaker Willy
Wonka is revealing his factory is run by an

00:08:10.309 --> 00:08:13.729
army of tiny green-haired men that are weirdly
inhuman.

00:08:13.729 --> 00:08:16.939
One minute the kids are all learning about
candy, and the next, one of them is being

00:08:16.939 --> 00:08:18.360
turned into a blueberry.

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:21.729
Another is left to nearly drown in a river
of chocolate, and then he takes everyone else

00:08:21.729 --> 00:08:25.169
through a tunnel of outright terror before
punishing some more of the kids.

00:08:25.169 --> 00:08:27.460
Like showing them a chicken snuff film wasn't
bad enough.

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:29.040
"This is kinda strange."

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:31.560
"Yes, strange, Charlie, but it's fun!"

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:33.520
Pee-Wee's Big Adventure

00:08:33.539 --> 00:08:37.240
The original Pee-Wee's Playhouse wasn't exactly
devoid of bizarre imagery to begin with.

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:38.240
"Aaaaargh!

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:41.440
Come in and pull yourself up a chair"

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:42.899
"A Chairy!"

00:08:42.899 --> 00:08:46.610
But under the guiding hand of the master of
strange, Tim Burton, Pee-Wee's Big Adventure

00:08:46.610 --> 00:08:48.750
became something deeply unsettling.

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:52.370
The film is chock-full of mastermind Paul
Reubens' signature weirdness, and Burton's

00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:55.019
direction cranks it up to the highest possible
level.

00:08:55.019 --> 00:08:59.310
It's perhaps no more evident anywhere in the
film than in the appearance of Large Marge,

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:02.680
a terrifying ghost trucker who gives Pee-Wee
a ride.

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:03.500
"This!"

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:05.380
"Ahhhh!"

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:08.940
The appeal of Pee-Wee Herman as a character
has always sort of been caught between adults

00:09:08.949 --> 00:09:12.839
and children, sometimes skewing heavily in
one direction and then suddenly leaning into

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:13.920
the opposite.

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:22.040
"Milk, milk, lemonade... round the corner
fudge is made."

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:25.740
The movie makes that switch frequently, which
just makes the more surreal Burton bits all

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:26.740
the scarier.

00:09:26.740 --> 00:09:28.910
One never quite knows what to expect.

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:31.500
Going Clear: Scientology and the Prison of
Belief

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:35.600
It's easy to joke about the Church of Scientology
thanks to a few pretty choice moments from

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:37.730
famous member Tom Cruise.

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:40.340
"Have you met an SP?"

00:09:43.029 --> 00:09:46.920
Going Clear serves as a tremendously important
reminder that the organization is far more

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:50.860
than a punchline—and that it's alleged to
inflict a very real horror on its members

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:52.190
and their loved ones daily.

00:09:52.190 --> 00:09:56.350
The filmmakers interview multiple former members
about their time in the organization and what

00:09:56.350 --> 00:09:58.760
they saw, and their stories will horrify you.

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:03.529
From cult-like groupthink to demeaning manual
labor, the stories get worse as the film progresses,

00:10:03.529 --> 00:10:07.639
with a couple of men who held high positions
in Scientology openly talking about using

00:10:07.639 --> 00:10:11.500
physical intimidation on detractors of the
church as well as its members.

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:15.110
But no segment of the film is as chilling
as the one in which a woman recounts the story

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:19.820
of the church effectively kidnapping her newborn
daughter, forcing her to do hard labor, and

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:23.649
what she subsequently went through to break
out of a church facility with her child and

00:10:23.649 --> 00:10:24.649
escape.

00:10:24.649 --> 00:10:27.649
Going Clear takes an organization many people
are loosely familiar with as a pop culture

00:10:27.649 --> 00:10:33.149
reference and recontextualizes it as the masterpiece
of a career con man, its founder, sci-fi author

00:10:33.149 --> 00:10:34.530
L. Ron Hubbard.

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:38.339
That his group has proven so successful even
decades after his passing, and that nobody

00:10:38.339 --> 00:10:42.829
seems to be capable of stopping them, may
be as frightening as any sci-fi novel.

00:10:42.829 --> 00:10:43.829
Thanks for watching!

00:10:43.829 --> 00:10:45.959
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:45.959 --> 00:10:48.230
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

